#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a38dba5-622e-5356-bae0-ac479825ebd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293836} {:answer/id #uuid "be64c842-1c2a-5e23-a999-aa07cfad96f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293837} {:answer/id #uuid "5c808dd9-e862-5d77-b630-69b56e7db2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293838} {:answer/id #uuid "381f926c-73d2-55a8-b374-e171f87db6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293839} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf16a51-e154-5a7d-95d8-b1d2a62fdb49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a38dba5-622e-5356-bae0-ac479825ebd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293836} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29de4f2-d3d7-5c89-9a1e-6f0bd70b60fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "625bbe54-0b3f-5afb-b15e-eb3a5b309cd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-114415" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1+2x){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({x}^{2}+x)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x+1){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+3x+1){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so there is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is false. $x=1$ is not a critical point.\n\n- Statement IV is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b0353e-bafe-5d57-8d37-72e1c92e74db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303567} {:answer/id #uuid "591ddb1c-6d16-5956-8f0e-ff28e8c470f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303568} {:answer/id #uuid "e4731eb3-c838-5d5f-a4b2-516b9d92f14e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303569} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb347c2-9856-5f04-8482-c7ada82795cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303570} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c3dfe8-712a-5dde-a801-493afa1e462a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffb347c2-9856-5f04-8482-c7ada82795cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303570} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f403874-b77e-5453-b0dd-fc42a08f3eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7424056-31be-5c03-b30d-76d3e1194cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-21500" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}+5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2}(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}=-\\frac{45}{4}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{-1/2},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=-\\frac{45}{4}(1)^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}(1)^{-1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f938ad-353c-563a-bb28-434637d997ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351696} {:answer/id #uuid "18f053ff-a1fe-5622-9c8d-98932373bb88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351697} {:answer/id #uuid "15f0c010-1361-5997-9f40-1d109164d664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351698} {:answer/id #uuid "a33c956c-2e8d-59a8-94af-bad5756927e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351699} {:answer/id #uuid "211a6fa5-53b7-5ba5-97e5-e0ef073485fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15f0c010-1361-5997-9f40-1d109164d664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351698} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6fbe6af-8468-576c-a07f-7773a87a6606" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "926334df-0b1b-522b-8706-62f62f0aec44" :question/math-academy-id "q-21521" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+24x-36.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(4)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(4)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=4$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=4$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+24x-36,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+24.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4) & =-3(4) \\\\  & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217bd976-896b-5452-b1ad-a3a847da13dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351703} {:answer/id #uuid "782bd92e-d7ba-5a6d-93ac-0b1097dbfaab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351704} {:answer/id #uuid "506869c7-6597-5de4-9a29-a8668746b2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351705} {:answer/id #uuid "43bd9121-1122-5bf5-bdfa-92ba248aa110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351706} {:answer/id #uuid "0977ef3a-d411-5be6-a292-8ee01ebcd3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "506869c7-6597-5de4-9a29-a8668746b2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351705} :answer-field/id #uuid "82a60489-ba67-56dc-bc34-5c843f940afd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab4d3059-294a-5af3-acf8-ea96870985be" :question/math-academy-id "q-22580" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-{e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /4}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ebe0146-c584-5f47-8010-3c8379701446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351710} {:answer/id #uuid "b563e8e5-dc73-5e8f-95c5-d976e23ee2ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351711} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dfa8c7-cab8-592d-9377-28775914c156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351712} {:answer/id #uuid "eda0df5e-de01-5e8a-a6e5-0b7767ed77e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351713} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4db8c8-7f26-5736-b2f9-10180aa560ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ebe0146-c584-5f47-8010-3c8379701446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351710} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef8313ce-ae4d-5118-8cbe-c50971c859f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8564b8fc-7dae-5753-9233-dc13c5a14ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-2471" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)<0$\n- $x=-2$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=15{x}^{2}+30x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{2}+30x & =0 \\\\ 15x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,-2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=30x+30,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =30>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =-30<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I is true while statement II is false. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is true while statement VI is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-2,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}]}
